{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795713682,"nonce":"B2pqm+CTjNkbeyeDZgzZAnak22I=","title":"test_imex_ignites_when_the_stiff_ode_does[ars443] passes with 0.17 percentage points to spare","message":"The ignition-delay check of the IMEX test uses a 1 % tolerance, and the ars443 scheme already uses 83 % of it, so a change that moves the converged Newton point by half a percent turns the test red.\n\n**What goes wrong.** `tests/test_solve_imex.py::test_imex_ignites_when_the_stiff_ode_does` marches a stiff ignition problem (A = 1e7, dt = 5e-3, a front about 1e-3 wide in time) and asserts `abs(got - want) \u003c 0.01 * want`, where `want` is the delay of a tight Radau reference and `got` the delay read off the saved steps by linear interpolation. Measured on main, CPU (float64): reference delay 0.55191; ars222 gives 0.55215 (0.04 % off, 4 % of the tolerance); ars443 gives 0.54730 (0.83 % off, 83 % of the tolerance). The same ars443 run on a GPU gives 0.547303, so ordinary rounding differences are harmless (4e-6 relative). A change of the block Newton solve to a closed-form inverse, tried while working on another issue, moved the ars443 delay on the GPU to 0.5450 (1.25 % off), which fails the assertion although nothing is wrong with the scheme: the delay moved by 0.4 % because the iteration stops at its tolerance, so the converged point depends on the solve path at that level.\n\n**Reproduce.** `pytest \"tests/test_solve_imex.py::test_imex_ignites_when_the_stiff_ode_does\"`; then read `got` and `want` (as above) for each method.\n\n**Expected.** A tolerance that separates \"the scheme is wrong\" from \"the scheme at dt = 5e-3 is a step-fraction off\". The delay resolution is one step (dt / want = 0.9 %), and the scheme's own error is 0.83 %, so 1 % is close to one step and is not a measure of the scheme's accuracy. A correct implementation can fail it, and a broken one within a step can pass it.\n\n**Where.** `tests/test_solve_imex.py`: `test_imex_ignites_when_the_stiff_ode_does` (the assertion `abs(got - want) \u003c 0.01 * want`); the Newton block solve of the IMEX stage in `jno/solve.py` / its solver module (the stopping tolerance decides how far the delay moves).\n\n**Workaround.** None needed on main today; a change to the stage solve may need the tolerance first.\n\n**Direction.** Replace the single-dt absolute check by a convergence check (the delay error at dt and dt/2 drops by about the scheme's order), or compare against the same scheme at a tight Newton tolerance, or at least widen to about 2 % with a comment that it is one-step resolution. Also look at whether the stage Newton tolerance should be tied to the step's rtol. Mark as CI.","files":null}]}